==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|prævail|prævaile|prævale||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂welh₁-|id=rule}}
From {{inh|en|enm|prevailen}}, from {{der|en|fro|prevaler}}, from {{der|en|la|praevaleō||be very able or more able, be superior, prevail}}, from {{m|la|prae||before}} + {{m|la|valeō||be able or powerful}}. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|rīcsian}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pɹɪˈveɪl/|a=RP,GA,CA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-prevail.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/pɹɪˈvæɪl/|a=AU}}
* {{IPA|en|/pɹəˈvæɪl/|a=NZ}}
* {{hyphenation|en|pre|vail}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪl|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[superior]] in [[strength]], [[dominance]], [[influence]]{{,}} or [[frequency]]; to have or gain the [[advantage]] over others; to [[have the upper hand]]; to [[outnumber]] others.
#: {{ux|en|Red colour '''prevails''' in the Canadian flag.}}
#: {{ux|en|Sunny skies will '''prevail''' across the Northeast.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Exodus|17|11|text=And it came to pass, when Moses held up his hand, that Israel '''prevailed'''; and when he let down his hand, Amalek '''prevailed'''.}}
#* {{quote-web
|en
|date=2022-02-27
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=Chelsea 0-0 Liverpool
|work=w:BBC Sport
|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/60450274
|passage=Liverpool created a host of chances and had a {{w|Joel Matip}} goal ruled out for a foul and offside in an incident-packed game that went right down to the wire before {{w|Jurgen Klopp}}'s side '''prevailed'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261005230716/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/60450274|archivedate=5 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[triumph]]; to be [[victorious]].
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Nick Paton Walsh and Katharina Krebs|title=MI6 chief makes open plea to Russians to spy for UK|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/07/19/europe/mi6-russia-spy-plea-uk-intl|date=2023-07-19|passage=Zakharova also made light of Moore’s claims that Russia will not be able to regain momentum in the war after the MI6 chief said he was”optimistic”{{sic}} Ukraine would '''prevail''' against the Russian invasion.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250927112912/https://www.cnn.com/2023/07/19/europe/mi6-russia-spy-plea-uk-intl|archivedate=27 September 2025}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[current]], [[widespread]]{{,}} or [[predominant]]; to have [[currency]] or [[prevalence]].
#: {{syn|en|obtain}}
#: {{ux|en|In his day and age, such practices '''prevailed''' all over Europe.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|often with '''''upon''''' or '''''on'''''}} To succeed in [[persuade|persuading]] or [[induce|inducing]].
#: {{ux|en|I '''prevailed''' on him to wait.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|passage=Jones began to be very importunate with the lady to unmask; and at length having '''prevailed''', there appeared not Mrs Fitzpatrick, but the Lady Bellaston herself.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[avail]].

